{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-159749",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=3,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =(-3)^{2}\\cdot 5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =45+12-24 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(11,33).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
